Every question must include at least two concrete situational details: a specific symptom/problem, a constraint or observed behavior, and a requested practical decision. Do not write vague prompts such as you are tasked with optimizing a pipeline without describing the actual problem. Every question must test a practical scenario. Do not generate Easy questions. Never ask definitions or isolated facts (e.g. What is a Pod?).
Medium (moderate): multi-step practical application, debugging, troubleshooting and interacting JD requirements. Hard: complex root-cause analysis, design, performance, risks and trade-offs where relevant to the JD.
Use ONLY experience required by the JD to calibrate complexity: 0-2 years practical fundamentals; 3-5 moderate multi-step work; 6-8 advanced troubleshooting, design and ownership; 8+ complex incidents and technical decisions. If experience is absent, return Not specified; do not invent years or requirements.
Use only technologies explicitly mentioned or unavoidable for an explicit JD requirement. Do not introduce unrelated technologies in questions, answers, distractors or starter code.
Convert responsibilities into realistic situations; include varied scenario structures. No exact, semantic, scenario or pattern duplicates.
Every assessment MUST use this fixed format regardless of role: exactly 10 MCQs (4 Medium/moderate and 6 Hard), exactly 2 Medium/moderate CODING questions, and exactly 2 Medium/moderate SQL questions, totaling {{ total_questions }} questions. Order Q001-Q010 MCQ, Q011-Q012 CODING, Q013-Q014 SQL. No Easy questions and no substitutions.
The requested coding and SQL formats are mandatory even if the JD does not explicitly list those formats. Keep the tested responsibility/skill grounded in the posted JD. For coding use a language/tool stated or unavoidably implied by the JD; if no language is stated, use language-neutral pseudocode and do not introduce Python by default. For SQL use role-relevant fictional tables to represent JD responsibilities; SQL is explicitly authorized as the assessment format, but do not introduce other unrelated technologies or job requirements. Skill tags and supporting references must still use real JD wording.
Coding: provide a concrete implementation/debugging task, inputs and expected outputs, constraints, and at least two example/edge cases. correct_answer must contain a reference implementation or pseudocode solution; include role-appropriate starter_code. SQL: include complete table schemas and a practical query task, handling a relevant data edge case; correct_answer must contain the reference SQL query. MCQs must be decision-based scenarios with plausible alternatives.
Mandatory skills and responsibilities must receive most questions; optional skills may receive at most 2 of the 14 questions. Shared skills are fine, but every question must have distinct situations, root causes and expected decisions. Do not invent new role requirements to make a scenario difficult. For every question provide a verbatim jd_requirement excerpt from the complete JD, a skill actually present there, explanation, scenario_type, evaluation_focus, and the correct answer. MCQ distractors must be plausible competing investigations or implementation choices. Never use ignore the issue, stop all work, delete everything, or obviously irrelevant actions as filler. MCQs have exactly 4 distinct, plausible options and one best answer whose text exactly matches an option. Answers must be correct and explanations must justify them.
